;; /*
;; Package rangefunc rewrites range-over-func to code that doesn't use range-over-funcs.
;; Rewriting the construct in the front end, before noder, means the functions generated during
;; the rewrite are available in a noder-generated representation for inlining by the back end.
;; 
;; # Theory of Operation
;; 
;; The basic idea is to rewrite
;; 
;; for x := range f {
;; ...
;; }
;; 
;; into
;; 
;; f(func(x T) bool {
;; ...
;; })
;; 
;; But it's not usually that easy.
;; 
;; # Range variables
;; 
;; For a range not using :=, the assigned variables cannot be function parameters
;; in the generated body function. Instead, we allocate fake parameters and
;; start the body with an assignment. For example:
;; 
;; for expr1, expr2 = range f {
;; ...
;; }
;; 
;; becomes
;; 
;; f(func(#p1 T1, #p2 T2) bool {
;; expr1, expr2 = #p1, #p2
;; ...
;; })
;; 
;; (All the generated variables have a # at the start to signal that they
;; are internal variables when looking at the generated code in a
;; debugger. Because variables have all been resolved to the specific
;; objects they represent, there is no danger of using plain "p1" and
;; colliding with a Go variable named "p1"; the # is just nice to have,
;; not for correctness.)
;; 
;; It can also happen that there are fewer range variables than function
;; arguments, in which case we end up with something like
;; 
;; f(func(x T1, _ T2) bool {
;; ...
;; })
;; 
;; or
;; 
;; f(func(#p1 T1, #p2 T2, _ T3) bool {
;; expr1, expr2 = #p1, #p2
;; ...
;; })
;; 
;; # Return
;; 
;; If the body contains a "break", that break turns into "return false",
;; to tell f to stop. And if the body contains a "continue", that turns
;; into "return true", to tell f to proceed with the next value.
;; Those are the easy cases.
;; 
;; If the body contains a return or a break/continue/goto L, then we need
;; to rewrite that into code that breaks out of the loop and then
;; triggers that control flow. In general we rewrite
;; 
;; for x := range f {
;; ...
;; }
;; 
;; into
;; 
;; {
;; var #next int
;; f(func(x T1) bool {
;; ...
;; return true
;; })
;; ... check #next ...
;; }
;; 
;; The variable #next is an integer code that says what to do when f
;; returns. Each difficult statement sets #next and then returns false to
;; stop f.
;; 
;; A plain "return" rewrites to {#next = -1; return false}.
;; The return false breaks the loop. Then when f returns, the "check
;; #next" section includes
;; 
;; if #next == -1 { return }
;; 
;; which causes the return we want.
;; 
;; Return with arguments is more involved, and has to deal with
;; corner cases involving panic, defer, and recover.  The results
;; of the enclosing function or closure are rewritten to give them
;; names if they don't have them already, and the names are assigned
;; at the return site.
;; 
;; func foo() (#rv1 A, #rv2 B) {
;; 
;; {
;; var (
;; #next int
;; )
;; f(func(x T1) bool {
;; ...
;; {
;; // return a, b
;; #rv1, #rv2 = a, b
;; #next = -1
;; return false
;; }
;; ...
;; return true
;; })
;; if #next == -1 { return }
;; }
;; 
;; # Checking
;; 
;; To permit checking that an iterator is well-behaved -- that is, that
;; it does not call the loop body again after it has returned false or
;; after the entire loop has exited (it might retain a copy of the body
;; function, or pass it to another goroutine) -- each generated loop has
;; its own #stateK variable that is used to check for permitted call
;; patterns to the yield function for a loop body.
;; 
;; The state values are:
;; 
;; abi.RF_DONE = 0      // body of loop has exited in a non-panic way
;; abi.RF_READY = 1     // body of loop has not exited yet, is not running
;; abi.RF_PANIC = 2     // body of loop is either currently running, or has panicked
;; abi.RF_EXHAUSTED = 3 // iterator function call, e.g. f(func(x t){...}), returned so the sequence is "exhausted".
;; 
;; abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC = 4 // used to report errors.
;; 
;; The value of #stateK transitions
;; (1) before calling the iterator function,
;; 
;; var #stateN = abi.RF_READY
;; 
;; (2) after the iterator function call returns,
;; 
;; if #stateN == abi.RF_PANIC {
;; panic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))
;; }
;; #stateN = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED
;; 
;; (3) at the beginning of the iteration of the loop body,
;; 
;; if #stateN != abi.RF_READY { #stateN = abi.RF_PANIC ; runtime.panicrangestate(#stateN) }
;; #stateN = abi.RF_PANIC
;; // This is slightly rearranged below for better code generation.
;; 
;; (4) when loop iteration continues,
;; 
;; #stateN = abi.RF_READY
;; [return true]
;; 
;; (5) when control flow exits the loop body.
;; 
;; #stateN = abi.RF_DONE
;; [return false]
;; 
;; For example:
;; 
;; for x := range f {
;; ...
;; if ... { break }
;; ...
;; }
;; 
;; becomes
;; 
;; {
;; var #state1 = abi.RF_READY
;; f(func(x T1) bool {
;; if #state1 != abi.RF_READY { #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state1) }
;; #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC
;; ...
;; if ... { #state1 = abi.RF_DONE ; return false }
;; ...
;; #state1 = abi.RF_READY
;; return true
;; })
;; if #state1 == abi.RF_PANIC {
;; // the code for the loop body did not return normally
;; panic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))
;; }
;; #state1 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED
;; }
;; 
;; # Nested Loops
;; 
;; So far we've only considered a single loop. If a function contains a
;; sequence of loops, each can be translated individually. But loops can
;; be nested. It would work to translate the innermost loop and then
;; translate the loop around it, and so on, except that there'd be a lot
;; of rewriting of rewritten code and the overall traversals could end up
;; taking time quadratic in the depth of the nesting. To avoid all that,
;; we use a single rewriting pass that handles a top-most range-over-func
;; loop and all the range-over-func loops it contains at the same time.
;; 
;; If we need to return from inside a doubly-nested loop, the rewrites
;; above stay the same, but the check after the inner loop only says
;; 
;; if #next < 0 { return false }
;; 
;; to stop the outer loop so it can do the actual return. That is,
;; 
;; for range f {
;; for range g {
;; ...
;; return a, b
;; ...
;; }
;; }
;; 
;; becomes
;; 
;; {
;; var (
;; #next int
;; )
;; var #state1 = abi.RF_READY
;; f(func() bool {
;; if #state1 != abi.RF_READY { #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state1) }
;; #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC
;; var #state2 = abi.RF_READY
;; g(func() bool {
;; if #state2 != abi.RF_READY { #state2 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state2) }
;; ...
;; {
;; // return a, b
;; #rv1, #rv2 = a, b
;; #next = -1
;; #state2 = abi.RF_DONE
;; return false
;; }
;; ...
;; #state2 = abi.RF_READY
;; return true
;; })
;; if #state2 == abi.RF_PANIC {
;; panic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))
;; }
;; #state2 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED
;; if #next < 0 {
;; #state1 = abi.RF_DONE
;; return false
;; }
;; #state1 = abi.RF_READY
;; return true
;; })
;; if #state1 == abi.RF_PANIC {
;; panic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))
;; }
;; #state1 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED
;; if #next == -1 {
;; return
;; }
;; }
;; 
;; # Labeled break/continue of range-over-func loops
;; 
;; For a labeled break or continue of an outer range-over-func, we
;; use positive #next values.
;; 
;; Any such labeled break or continue
;; really means "do N breaks" or "do N breaks and 1 continue".
;; 
;; The positive #next value tells which level of loop N to target
;; with a break or continue, where perLoopStep*N means break out of
;; level N and perLoopStep*N-1 means continue into level N.  The
;; outermost loop has level 1, therefore #next == perLoopStep means
;; to break from the outermost loop, and #next == perLoopStep-1 means
;; to continue the outermost loop.
;; 
;; Loops that might need to propagate a labeled break or continue
;; add one or both of these to the #next checks:
;; 
;; // N == depth of this loop, one less than the one just exited.
;; if #next != 0 {
;; if #next >= perLoopStep*N-1 { // break or continue this loop
;; if #next >= perLoopStep*N+1 { // error checking
;; // TODO reason about what exactly can appear
;; // here given full  or partial checking.
;; runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_DONE)
;; }
;; rv := #next & 1 == 1 // code generates into #next&1
;; #next = 0
;; return rv
;; }
;; return false // or handle returns and gotos
;; }
;; 
;; For example (with perLoopStep == 2)
;; 
;; F: for range f { // 1, 2
;; for range g { // 3, 4
;; for range h {
;; ...
;; break F
;; ...
;; ...
;; continue F
;; ...
;; }
;; }
;; ...
;; }
;; 
;; becomes
;; 
;; {
;; var #next int
;; var #state1 = abi.RF_READY
;; f(func() { // 1,2
;; if #state1 != abi.RF_READY { #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state1) }
;; #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC
;; var #state2 = abi.RF_READY
;; g(func() { // 3,4
;; if #state2 != abi.RF_READY { #state2 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state2) }
;; #state2 = abi.RF_PANIC
;; var #state3 = abi.RF_READY
;; h(func() { // 5,6
;; if #state3 != abi.RF_READY { #state3 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state3) }
;; #state3 = abi.RF_PANIC
;; ...
;; {
;; // break F
;; #next = 2
;; #state3 = abi.RF_DONE
;; return false
;; }
;; ...
;; {
;; // continue F
;; #next = 1
;; #state3 = abi.RF_DONE
;; return false
;; }
;; ...
;; #state3 = abi.RF_READY
;; return true
;; })
;; if #state3 == abi.RF_PANIC {
;; panic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))
;; }
;; #state3 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED
;; if #next != 0 {
;; // no breaks or continues targeting this loop
;; #state2 = abi.RF_DONE
;; return false
;; }
;; return true
;; })
;; if #state2 == abi.RF_PANIC {
;; panic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))
;; }
;; #state2 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED
;; if #next != 0 { // just exited g, test for break/continue applied to f/F
;; if #next >= 1 {
;; if #next >= 3 { runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_DONE) } // error
;; rv := #next&1 == 1
;; #next = 0
;; return rv
;; }
;; #state1 = abi.RF_DONE
;; return false
;; }
;; ...
;; return true
;; })
;; if #state1 == abi.RF_PANIC {
;; panic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))
;; }
;; #state1 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED
;; }
;; 
;; Note that the post-h checks only consider a break,
;; since no generated code tries to continue g.
;; 
;; # Gotos and other labeled break/continue
;; 
;; The final control flow translations are goto and break/continue of a
;; non-range-over-func statement. In both cases, we may need to break
;; out of one or more range-over-func loops before we can do the actual
;; control flow statement. Each such break/continue/goto L statement is
;; assigned a unique negative #next value (since -1 is return). Then
;; the post-checks for a given loop test for the specific codes that
;; refer to labels directly targetable from that block. Otherwise, the
;; generic
;; 
;; if #next < 0 { return false }
;; 
;; check handles stopping the next loop to get one step closer to the label.
;; 
;; For example
;; 
;; Top: print("start\n")
;; for range f {
;; for range g {
;; ...
;; for range h {
;; ...
;; goto Top
;; ...
;; }
;; }
;; }
;; 
;; becomes
;; 
;; Top: print("start\n")
;; {
;; var #next int
;; var #state1 = abi.RF_READY
;; f(func() {
;; if #state1 != abi.RF_READY{ #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state1) }
;; #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC
;; var #state2 = abi.RF_READY
;; g(func() {
;; if #state2 != abi.RF_READY { #state2 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state2) }
;; #state2 = abi.RF_PANIC
;; ...
;; var #state3 bool = abi.RF_READY
;; h(func() {
;; if #state3 != abi.RF_READY { #state3 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state3) }
;; #state3 = abi.RF_PANIC
;; ...
;; {
;; // goto Top
;; #next = -3
;; #state3 = abi.RF_DONE
;; return false
;; }
;; ...
;; #state3 = abi.RF_READY
;; return true
;; })
;; if #state3 == abi.RF_PANIC {runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC)}
;; #state3 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED
;; if #next < 0 {
;; #state2 = abi.RF_DONE
;; return false
;; }
;; #state2 = abi.RF_READY
;; return true
;; })
;; if #state2 == abi.RF_PANIC {runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC)}
;; #state2 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED
;; if #next < 0 {
;; #state1 = abi.RF_DONE
;; return false
;; }
;; #state1 = abi.RF_READY
;; return true
;; })
;; if #state1 == abi.RF_PANIC {runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC)}
;; #state1 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED
;; if #next == -3 {
;; #next = 0
;; goto Top
;; }
;; }
;; 
;; Labeled break/continue to non-range-over-funcs are handled the same
;; way as goto.
;; 
;; # Defers
;; 
;; The last wrinkle is handling defer statements. If we have
;; 
;; for range f {
;; defer print("A")
;; }
;; 
;; we cannot rewrite that into
;; 
;; f(func() {
;; defer print("A")
;; })
;; 
;; because the deferred code will run at the end of the iteration, not
;; the end of the containing function. To fix that, the runtime provides
;; a special hook that lets us obtain a defer "token" representing the
;; outer function and then use it in a later defer to attach the deferred
;; code to that outer function.
;; 
;; Normally,
;; 
;; defer print("A")
;; 
;; compiles to
;; 
;; runtime.deferproc(func() { print("A") })
;; 
;; This changes in a range-over-func. For example:
;; 
;; for range f {
;; defer print("A")
;; }
;; 
;; compiles to
;; 
;; var #defers = runtime.deferrangefunc()
;; f(func() {
;; runtime.deferprocat(func() { print("A") }, #defers)
;; })
;; 
;; For this rewriting phase, we insert the explicit initialization of
;; #defers and then attach the #defers variable to the CallStmt
;; representing the defer. That variable will be propagated to the
;; backend and will cause the backend to compile the defer using
;; deferprocat instead of an ordinary deferproc.
;; 
;; TODO: Could call runtime.deferrangefuncend after f.
;; */
(in-ns 'go.cmd.compile.internal.rangefunc) (go/file "rewrite.go" :doc "Package rangefunc rewrites range-over-func to code that doesn't use range-over-funcs.\nRewriting the construct in the front end, before noder, means the functions generated during\nthe rewrite are available in a noder-generated representation for inlining by the back end.\n\n# Theory of Operation\n\nThe basic idea is to rewrite\n\n\tfor x := range f {\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\ninto\n\n\tf(func(x T) bool {\n\t\t...\n\t})\n\nBut it's not usually that easy.\n\n# Range variables\n\nFor a range not using :=, the assigned variables cannot be function parameters\nin the generated body function. Instead, we allocate fake parameters and\nstart the body with an assignment. For example:\n\n\tfor expr1, expr2 = range f {\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\nbecomes\n\n\tf(func(#p1 T1, #p2 T2) bool {\n\t\texpr1, expr2 = #p1, #p2\n\t\t...\n\t})\n\n(All the generated variables have a # at the start to signal that they\nare internal variables when looking at the generated code in a\ndebugger. Because variables have all been resolved to the specific\nobjects they represent, there is no danger of using plain \"p1\" and\ncolliding with a Go variable named \"p1\"; the # is just nice to have,\nnot for correctness.)\n\nIt can also happen that there are fewer range variables than function\narguments, in which case we end up with something like\n\n\tf(func(x T1, _ T2) bool {\n\t\t...\n\t})\n\nor\n\n\tf(func(#p1 T1, #p2 T2, _ T3) bool {\n\t\texpr1, expr2 = #p1, #p2\n\t\t...\n\t})\n\n# Return\n\nIf the body contains a \"break\", that break turns into \"return false\",\nto tell f to stop. And if the body contains a \"continue\", that turns\ninto \"return true\", to tell f to proceed with the next value.\nThose are the easy cases.\n\nIf the body contains a return or a break/continue/goto L, then we need\nto rewrite that into code that breaks out of the loop and then\ntriggers that control flow. In general we rewrite\n\n\tfor x := range f {\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\ninto\n\n\t{\n\t\tvar #next int\n\t\tf(func(x T1) bool {\n\t\t\t...\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t})\n\t\t... check #next ...\n\t}\n\nThe variable #next is an integer code that says what to do when f\nreturns. Each difficult statement sets #next and then returns false to\nstop f.\n\nA plain \"return\" rewrites to {#next = -1; return false}.\nThe return false breaks the loop. Then when f returns, the \"check\n#next\" section includes\n\n\tif #next == -1 { return }\n\nwhich causes the return we want.\n\nReturn with arguments is more involved, and has to deal with\ncorner cases involving panic, defer, and recover.  The results\nof the enclosing function or closure are rewritten to give them\nnames if they don't have them already, and the names are assigned\nat the return site.\n\n\t  func foo() (#rv1 A, #rv2 B) {\n\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tvar (\n\t\t\t\t#next int\n\t\t\t)\n\t\t\tf(func(x T1) bool {\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\t\t// return a, b\n\t\t\t\t\t#rv1, #rv2 = a, b\n\t\t\t\t\t#next = -1\n\t\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t\t})\n\t\t\tif #next == -1 { return }\n\t\t}\n\n# Checking\n\nTo permit checking that an iterator is well-behaved -- that is, that\nit does not call the loop body again after it has returned false or\nafter the entire loop has exited (it might retain a copy of the body\nfunction, or pass it to another goroutine) -- each generated loop has\nits own #stateK variable that is used to check for permitted call\npatterns to the yield function for a loop body.\n\nThe state values are:\n\nabi.RF_DONE = 0      // body of loop has exited in a non-panic way\nabi.RF_READY = 1     // body of loop has not exited yet, is not running\nabi.RF_PANIC = 2     // body of loop is either currently running, or has panicked\nabi.RF_EXHAUSTED = 3 // iterator function call, e.g. f(func(x t){...}), returned so the sequence is \"exhausted\".\n\nabi.RF_MISSING_PANIC = 4 // used to report errors.\n\nThe value of #stateK transitions\n(1) before calling the iterator function,\n\n\tvar #stateN = abi.RF_READY\n\n(2) after the iterator function call returns,\n\n\tif #stateN == abi.RF_PANIC {\n\t\tpanic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))\n\t}\n\t#stateN = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED\n\n(3) at the beginning of the iteration of the loop body,\n\n\tif #stateN != abi.RF_READY { #stateN = abi.RF_PANIC ; runtime.panicrangestate(#stateN) }\n\t#stateN = abi.RF_PANIC\n\t// This is slightly rearranged below for better code generation.\n\n(4) when loop iteration continues,\n\n\t#stateN = abi.RF_READY\n\t[return true]\n\n(5) when control flow exits the loop body.\n\n\t#stateN = abi.RF_DONE\n\t[return false]\n\nFor example:\n\n\tfor x := range f {\n\t\t...\n\t\tif ... { break }\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\nbecomes\n\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tvar #state1 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\tf(func(x T1) bool {\n\t\t\t\tif #state1 != abi.RF_READY { #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state1) }\n\t\t\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_PANIC\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\tif ... { #state1 = abi.RF_DONE ; return false }\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t\t})\n\t        if #state1 == abi.RF_PANIC {\n\t        \t// the code for the loop body did not return normally\n\t        \tpanic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))\n\t        }\n\t\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED\n\t\t}\n\n# Nested Loops\n\nSo far we've only considered a single loop. If a function contains a\nsequence of loops, each can be translated individually. But loops can\nbe nested. It would work to translate the innermost loop and then\ntranslate the loop around it, and so on, except that there'd be a lot\nof rewriting of rewritten code and the overall traversals could end up\ntaking time quadratic in the depth of the nesting. To avoid all that,\nwe use a single rewriting pass that handles a top-most range-over-func\nloop and all the range-over-func loops it contains at the same time.\n\nIf we need to return from inside a doubly-nested loop, the rewrites\nabove stay the same, but the check after the inner loop only says\n\n\tif #next < 0 { return false }\n\nto stop the outer loop so it can do the actual return. That is,\n\n\tfor range f {\n\t\tfor range g {\n\t\t\t...\n\t\t\treturn a, b\n\t\t\t...\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\nbecomes\n\n\t{\n\t\tvar (\n\t\t\t#next int\n\t\t)\n\t\tvar #state1 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\tf(func() bool {\n\t\t\tif #state1 != abi.RF_READY { #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state1) }\n\t\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_PANIC\n\t\t\tvar #state2 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\tg(func() bool {\n\t\t\t\tif #state2 != abi.RF_READY { #state2 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state2) }\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\t\t// return a, b\n\t\t\t\t\t#rv1, #rv2 = a, b\n\t\t\t\t\t#next = -1\n\t\t\t\t\t#state2 = abi.RF_DONE\n\t\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\t#state2 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t\t})\n\t        if #state2 == abi.RF_PANIC {\n\t        \tpanic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))\n\t        }\n\t\t\t#state2 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED\n\t\t\tif #next < 0 {\n\t\t\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_DONE\n\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t})\n\t    if #state1 == abi.RF_PANIC {\n\t       \tpanic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))\n\t    }\n\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED\n\t\tif #next == -1 {\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n# Labeled break/continue of range-over-func loops\n\nFor a labeled break or continue of an outer range-over-func, we\nuse positive #next values.\n\nAny such labeled break or continue\nreally means \"do N breaks\" or \"do N breaks and 1 continue\".\n\nThe positive #next value tells which level of loop N to target\nwith a break or continue, where perLoopStep*N means break out of\nlevel N and perLoopStep*N-1 means continue into level N.  The\noutermost loop has level 1, therefore #next == perLoopStep means\nto break from the outermost loop, and #next == perLoopStep-1 means\nto continue the outermost loop.\n\nLoops that might need to propagate a labeled break or continue\nadd one or both of these to the #next checks:\n\n\t    // N == depth of this loop, one less than the one just exited.\n\t\tif #next != 0 {\n\t\t  if #next >= perLoopStep*N-1 { // break or continue this loop\n\t\t  \tif #next >= perLoopStep*N+1 { // error checking\n\t\t  \t   // TODO reason about what exactly can appear\n\t\t  \t   // here given full  or partial checking.\n\t           runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_DONE)\n\t\t  \t}\n\t\t  \trv := #next & 1 == 1 // code generates into #next&1\n\t\t\t#next = 0\n\t\t\treturn rv\n\t\t  }\n\t\t  return false // or handle returns and gotos\n\t\t}\n\nFor example (with perLoopStep == 2)\n\n\tF: for range f { // 1, 2\n\t\tfor range g { // 3, 4\n\t\t\tfor range h {\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\tbreak F\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\tcontinue F\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\nbecomes\n\n\t{\n\t\tvar #next int\n\t\tvar #state1 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\tf(func() { // 1,2\n\t\t\tif #state1 != abi.RF_READY { #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state1) }\n\t\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_PANIC\n\t\t\tvar #state2 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\tg(func() { // 3,4\n\t\t\t\tif #state2 != abi.RF_READY { #state2 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state2) }\n\t\t\t\t#state2 = abi.RF_PANIC\n\t\t\t\tvar #state3 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\t\th(func() { // 5,6\n\t\t\t\t\tif #state3 != abi.RF_READY { #state3 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state3) }\n\t\t\t\t\t#state3 = abi.RF_PANIC\n\t\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\t\t\t// break F\n\t\t\t\t\t\t#next = 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t#state3 = abi.RF_DONE\n\t\t\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\t\t\t// continue F\n\t\t\t\t\t\t#next = 1\n\t\t\t\t\t\t#state3 = abi.RF_DONE\n\t\t\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\t\t#state3 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t\t\t})\n\t\t\t\tif #state3 == abi.RF_PANIC {\n\t\t\t\t\tpanic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t#state3 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED\n\t\t\t\tif #next != 0 {\n\t\t\t\t\t// no breaks or continues targeting this loop\n\t\t\t\t\t#state2 = abi.RF_DONE\n\t\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t\t})\n\t    \tif #state2 == abi.RF_PANIC {\n\t       \t\tpanic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))\n\t   \t\t}\n\t\t\t#state2 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED\n\t\t\tif #next != 0 { // just exited g, test for break/continue applied to f/F\n\t\t\t\tif #next >= 1 {\n\t\t\t\t\tif #next >= 3 { runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_DONE) } // error\n\t\t\t\t\trv := #next&1 == 1\n\t\t\t\t\t#next = 0\n\t\t\t\t\treturn rv\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_DONE\n\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t...\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t})\n\t    if #state1 == abi.RF_PANIC {\n\t       \tpanic(runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC))\n\t    }\n\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED\n\t}\n\nNote that the post-h checks only consider a break,\nsince no generated code tries to continue g.\n\n# Gotos and other labeled break/continue\n\nThe final control flow translations are goto and break/continue of a\nnon-range-over-func statement. In both cases, we may need to break\nout of one or more range-over-func loops before we can do the actual\ncontrol flow statement. Each such break/continue/goto L statement is\nassigned a unique negative #next value (since -1 is return). Then\nthe post-checks for a given loop test for the specific codes that\nrefer to labels directly targetable from that block. Otherwise, the\ngeneric\n\n\tif #next < 0 { return false }\n\ncheck handles stopping the next loop to get one step closer to the label.\n\nFor example\n\n\tTop: print(\"start\\n\")\n\tfor range f {\n\t\tfor range g {\n\t\t\t...\n\t\t\tfor range h {\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\tgoto Top\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\nbecomes\n\n\tTop: print(\"start\\n\")\n\t{\n\t\tvar #next int\n\t\tvar #state1 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\tf(func() {\n\t\t\tif #state1 != abi.RF_READY{ #state1 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state1) }\n\t\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_PANIC\n\t\t\tvar #state2 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\tg(func() {\n\t\t\t\tif #state2 != abi.RF_READY { #state2 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state2) }\n\t\t\t\t#state2 = abi.RF_PANIC\n\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\tvar #state3 bool = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\t\th(func() {\n\t\t\t\t\tif #state3 != abi.RF_READY { #state3 = abi.RF_PANIC; runtime.panicrangestate(#state3) }\n\t\t\t\t\t#state3 = abi.RF_PANIC\n\t\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\t\t{\n\t\t\t\t\t\t// goto Top\n\t\t\t\t\t\t#next = -3\n\t\t\t\t\t\t#state3 = abi.RF_DONE\n\t\t\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t\t...\n\t\t\t\t\t#state3 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t\t\t})\n\t\t\t\tif #state3 == abi.RF_PANIC {runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC)}\n\t\t\t\t#state3 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED\n\t\t\t\tif #next < 0 {\n\t\t\t\t\t#state2 = abi.RF_DONE\n\t\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t#state2 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t\t})\n\t\t\tif #state2 == abi.RF_PANIC {runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC)}\n\t\t\t#state2 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED\n\t\t\tif #next < 0 {\n\t\t\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_DONE\n\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_READY\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t})\n\t\tif #state1 == abi.RF_PANIC {runtime.panicrangestate(abi.RF_MISSING_PANIC)}\n\t\t#state1 = abi.RF_EXHAUSTED\n\t\tif #next == -3 {\n\t\t\t#next = 0\n\t\t\tgoto Top\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\nLabeled break/continue to non-range-over-funcs are handled the same\nway as goto.\n\n# Defers\n\nThe last wrinkle is handling defer statements. If we have\n\n\tfor range f {\n\t\tdefer print(\"A\")\n\t}\n\nwe cannot rewrite that into\n\n\tf(func() {\n\t\tdefer print(\"A\")\n\t})\n\nbecause the deferred code will run at the end of the iteration, not\nthe end of the containing function. To fix that, the runtime provides\na special hook that lets us obtain a defer \"token\" representing the\nouter function and then use it in a later defer to attach the deferred\ncode to that outer function.\n\nNormally,\n\n\tdefer print(\"A\")\n\ncompiles to\n\n\truntime.deferproc(func() { print(\"A\") })\n\nThis changes in a range-over-func. For example:\n\n\tfor range f {\n\t\tdefer print(\"A\")\n\t}\n\ncompiles to\n\n\tvar #defers = runtime.deferrangefunc()\n\tf(func() {\n\t\truntime.deferprocat(func() { print(\"A\") }, #defers)\n\t})\n\nFor this rewriting phase, we insert the explicit initialization of\n#defers and then attach the #defers variable to the CallStmt\nrepresenting the defer. That variable will be propagated to the\nbackend and will cause the backend to compile the defer using\ndeferprocat instead of an ordinary deferproc.\n\nTODO: Could call runtime.deferrangefuncend after f.\n" :imports [


